  'Survey of Spanish (Peninsular) Literature',
  'Students identify major periods, genres, authors, debates, and cultural contexts in Peninsular Spanish literary history, and analyze literary form, language, genre, and narrative or poetic technique in Spanish with historically grounded close reading. They explain how Iberian multilingualism, religion, empire, class, gender, region, war, dictatorship, democracy, and memory shape literary production, connect canonical texts to canon formation and marginalized voices, and support an original historical-literary argument in writing and oral presentation.',
  $penljson${"slug": "survey-of-spanish-peninsular-literature", "title": "Survey of Spanish (Peninsular) Literature", "description": "Students identify major periods, genres, authors, debates, and cultural contexts in Peninsular Spanish literary history, and analyze literary form, language, genre, and narrative or poetic technique in Spanish with historically grounded close reading. They explain how Iberian multilingualism, religion, empire, class, gender, region, war, dictatorship, democracy, and memory shape literary production, connect canonical texts to canon formation and marginalized voices, and support an original historical-literary argument in writing and oral presentation.", "topics": [{"id": "6d1ec3f1-f387-4592-a221-b23e11af9f12", "type": "topic", "title": "Literary Survey Methods, Iberian Contexts, and Periodization", "description": "This opening unit teaches students how a literary survey is constructed. Rather than treating periods as neutral containers, students learn to ask who names periods, whose texts become canonical, and how multilingual Iberia complicates a single national story.", "children": [{"id": "73ba1d71-837c-4dde-9eed-837d149df29e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Survey method and literary history", "children": [{"id": "1577f2b5-7f99-44a1-b17d-a7db1dab75da", "type": "concept", "title": "period labels"}, {"id": "7e090ec2-4084-44c0-8598-ba369f189dc1", "type": "concept", "title": "genre history"}, {"id": "d52dbe3a-cac3-4c7b-a31c-29324cb7aba6", "type": "concept", "title": "canon formation"}, {"id": "647d4ee8-f70c-4c28-a1e8-317f09861ee4", "type": "concept", "title": "survey as argument"}], "description": "Students learn the purpose and limits of moving chronologically across centuries of literature."}, {"id": "b9031b50-33bc-4e12-b167-162376cdfe25", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Iberian multilingual and multicultural contexts", "children": [{"id": "74529617-c84c-4d1e-a7c3-ac06aeead578", "type": "concept", "title": "Castilian, Catalan, Galician, Basque"}, {"id": "4b7fdeca-aaad-47e6-8efb-07c763f63fd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Arabic and Hebrew legacies"}, {"id": "1547c957-f9bf-4748-bc3d-2027fbf9ae50", "type": "concept", "title": "religious plurality and conflict"}, {"id": "6bdad3c8-5771-49f9-a40e-316fa7499084", "type": "concept", "title": "regional identity"}], "description": "The point is to frame Spanish literature within a peninsula shaped by multiple languages, religions, regions, and political projects."}, {"id": "a5eda970-ba79-43bc-8c35-1c1dcd8827e2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Tools for literary analysis", "children": [{"id": "6997149a-4b7c-43b2-9716-6d89aee757e5", "type": "concept", "title": "speaker and narrator"}, {"id": "1f46ba13-a98b-4d50-89a8-738f711e98ea", "type": "concept", "title": "genre and form"}, {"id": "4ac16d2a-958f-443e-a248-0475a951721e", "type": "concept", "title": "meter and rhetoric"}, {"id": "28cdbf05-3b85-4471-ae74-8fb8e7b849ec", "type": "concept", "title": "historical context"}], "description": "Students build a shared vocabulary for close reading across poetry, prose, theater, and essay."}]}, {"id": "ebe2913e-7c39-4eb2-9ed2-10119f265576", "type": "topic", "title": "Medieval Iberian Texts", "description": "Medieval texts introduce students to manuscript culture, oral tradition, religious encounter, and early vernacular literary forms. The unit resists treating the Middle Ages as primitive background and instead emphasizes complex cultural production.", "children": [{"id": "f39f2955-9bce-4b14-b5b3-ba61b3f9cbb4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Epic, lyric, and oral tradition", "children": [{"id": "023cf275-80c1-4f32-9210-1c77f3cb212b", "type": "concept", "title": "Cantar de mio Cid"}, {"id": "f6ddea96-f6da-4e6f-8557-911a1bade7e3", "type": "concept", "title": "jarchas and lyric traditions"}, {"id": "74001453-edef-4150-9fa3-3e1aa80b7a76", "type": "concept", "title": "honor and exile"}, {"id": "429e84af-b7d7-4330-98f6-c5b4e62a00ff", "type": "concept", "title": "orality and manuscript transmission"}], "description": "Students learn how performance, memory, and social values shape early literary forms."}, {"id": "88b9d819-4194-4e2c-974d-e6f39c0d8020", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religious and didactic writing", "children": [{"id": "cdde8e7c-187e-482d-b227-11001f8e17f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Gonzalo de Berceo"}, {"id": "73fc1466-17f9-4dbe-8878-206d1a11dd69", "type": "concept", "title": "exempla"}, {"id": "2255f095-4c6f-4f87-bd6e-789b5d87f15e", "type": "concept", "title": "Libro de buen amor"}, {"id": "59b6ff85-8951-464a-97b0-4d2c427f873a", "type": "concept", "title": "allegory and instruction"}], "description": "The goal is to understand how literature teaches, persuades, entertains, and organizes moral knowledge."}, {"id": "c73bfdac-72ce-4e12-9301-c411e37b0003", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Convivencia and its critiques", "children": [{"id": "e5e0821c-b287-4cc9-92f4-907a95862628", "type": "concept", "title": "Christian, Muslim, Jewish contexts"}, {"id": "e532329a-be0c-4ad8-84b8-52bf23c8db04", "type": "concept", "title": "translation culture"}, {"id": "44aabd6f-bb61-43f4-8167-d973305f2fcc", "type": "concept", "title": "violence and conversion"}, {"id": "287f1cbe-375d-44fa-827d-b2cee11ca18e", "type": "concept", "title": "historiographic debate"}], "description": "Students examine coexistence and conflict without romanticizing medieval Iberia."}]}, {"id": "1e0dab68-0ceb-444d-b0df-eb4ff89d29c9", "type": "topic", "title": "Early Modern Spain and the Golden Age", "description": "This unit links literary innovation to empire, print, religious control, social mobility, and theatrical culture. Students see the Golden Age as both dazzling artistic production and a period of contradiction.", "children": [{"id": "98d70f78-d9c5-4d6f-8009-3e650725d2b4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Empire, print, and social order", "children": [{"id": "9c6762cb-c169-413e-9ceb-ccec118a4a33", "type": "concept", "title": "Habsburg empire"}, {"id": "e429db1b-df68-4508-a0a4-635bcad0ae06", "type": "concept", "title": "Inquisition"}, {"id": "4bdbad85-a534-406e-862f-e234f16c0ff4", "type": "concept", "title": "limpieza de sangre"}, {"id": "80e0b484-43fb-4e00-91b3-350e206bd786", "type": "concept", "title": "print and readership"}], "description": "Students learn the institutions and anxieties that shape early modern writing."}, {"id": "15e1de85-fe7f-4f46-8336-bfea449b05f3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Picaresque and social critique", "children": [{"id": "1b57bfa7-292f-42c8-beb4-a43143ea79c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Lazarillo de Tormes"}, {"id": "5e77f6fa-6e20-45ce-a7c4-15535a6275a1", "type": "concept", "title": "antihero"}, {"id": "5aa862b3-9dbc-407f-bdc7-21c2566b6686", "type": "concept", "title": "autobiographical fiction"}, {"id": "2dd2eb7b-57a9-4857-9a9b-82151e7fea30", "type": "concept", "title": "hunger and service"}], "description": "The picaresque exposes poverty, hypocrisy, mobility, and narrative self-fashioning."}, {"id": "db31b47b-f96b-4085-985e-8803e06d6329", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender, honor, and theatrical publics", "children": [{"id": "705a786a-75ac-4960-ac93-97b4c5a6acea", "type": "concept", "title": "honor culture"}, {"id": "09048075-7ef7-42f5-930e-749fc61e1b51", "type": "concept", "title": "women on stage and page"}, {"id": "9d895ba2-12bd-4499-97b9-bc389c504ce0", "type": "concept", "title": "urban theater"}, {"id": "2db0570d-52d6-4271-b552-21018560130a", "type": "concept", "title": "comedia audience"}], "description": "Students connect literary forms to social codes and public performance."}]}, {"id": "85d9ac7e-baf3-4ac7-949d-4dc75ffcb69a", "type": "topic", "title": "Cervantes and the Novel", "description": "Cervantes becomes a laboratory for fiction: unreliable narration, parody, authorship, embedded stories, and the relation between reading and reality. Students study why Don Quijote remains central while also questioning what that centrality means.", "children": [{"id": "177b25e0-b14d-49b1-a467-15716455f68f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parody and genre consciousness", "children": [{"id": "3e0d5ea0-d640-43a8-af5b-510a198699bb", "type": "concept", "title": "books of chivalry"}, {"id": "8b1c7583-9746-4a1a-8bb6-c33672259483", "type": "concept", "title": "comic inversion"}, {"id": "35a0a975-c701-4c7b-9bf1-5ccfe502d7eb", "type": "concept", "title": "genre mixing"}, {"id": "031590cf-7c8d-4614-803d-0ddebbc6d982", "type": "concept", "title": "reader expectations"}], "description": "Students learn how Cervantes transforms chivalric romance and earlier narrative traditions."}, {"id": "9a584036-00ce-4104-bf2c-4744a886eb70", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Narration, authorship, and metafiction", "children": [{"id": "ab82a473-5b0d-492f-9287-6953f7a0759d", "type": "concept", "title": "Cide Hamete Benengeli"}, {"id": "069503bd-0e38-44e5-9109-bed21b2aea4f", "type": "concept", "title": "unreliable narration"}, {"id": "70aa04e0-a693-4ddd-8d1a-920113865ccb", "type": "concept", "title": "manuscript fiction"}, {"id": "b217fac0-916b-436d-b975-9f0097a16091", "type": "concept", "title": "embedded narratives"}], "description": "The point is to understand how the novel thinks about its own making."}, {"id": "d95ddab1-5667-49fd-95d0-a270bcbf6c59", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modernity and interpretation", "children": [{"id": "2b68e168-3e66-45c3-81db-88b9c35eb7fb", "type": "concept", "title": "subjectivity"}, {"id": "acab9c21-4773-409d-9ff1-25f05c5d79e8", "type": "concept", "title": "madness and reading"}, {"id": "c02938b4-3947-4858-8502-552bcd13bccd", "type": "concept", "title": "ethics of laughter"}, {"id": "cdab981c-4b54-4bdc-ba5d-36b40912b200", "type": "concept", "title": "canon and world literature"}], "description": "Students debate why Cervantes is linked to the modern novel and what that claim includes or excludes."}]}, {"id": "5a122cf3-fe0d-45c3-b913-27b2f590d855", "type": "topic", "title": "Baroque Poetry and Theater", "description": "Baroque literature intensifies difficulty, spectacle, and contradiction. Students learn to read dense poetry and theatrical conflict as responses to crisis, power, desire, and religious-political order.", "children": [{"id": "beb9bf2f-c0d4-4f06-b700-413a67889f0a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conceptismo and culteranismo", "children": [{"id": "25342a22-37cd-4430-a0f0-ad78ff257ea8", "type": "concept", "title": "Quevedo"}, {"id": "b033bff3-1972-4752-be31-dae0e43cdf9f", "type": "concept", "title": "Góngora"}, {"id": "7c072b7d-6237-473a-a188-5a8283b0efcd", "type": "concept", "title": "wit and compression"}, {"id": "751bb32a-7b54-4c1a-b329-0f10357b7f43", "type": "concept", "title": "syntax and ornament"}], "description": "Students learn the major poetic styles and why difficulty can be an aesthetic and social strategy."}, {"id": "970ad9d9-d568-448d-ae7e-22a3848bfc88", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The comedia and dramatic form", "children": [{"id": "15f6a251-5eee-46ab-9a5c-a6f5d93a25fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Lope de Vega"}, {"id": "a670490c-46c2-4804-8ade-4ba8a6f87e3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Calderón"}, {"id": "101f0d68-b56c-4800-ac87-5e3320eacdb5", "type": "concept", "title": "three-act structure"}, {"id": "fe6e50af-0e28-4bbc-aa05-c8619ba28de9", "type": "concept", "title": "gracioso and honor plots"}], "description": "The goal is to understand theater as literary text, performance, and institution."}, {"id": "e356688b-485d-4c4f-80d7-65f16bfdc5e1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Power, gender, and spectacle", "children": [{"id": "2f94c829-4535-4dec-b42d-54430a01c458", "type": "concept", "title": "honor and violence"}, {"id": "1cd52056-96eb-4999-969f-1bf05298d0ce", "type": "concept", "title": "female agency"}, {"id": "8db70c27-9884-49e9-b595-588a670f9001", "type": "concept", "title": "monarchy and theology"}, {"id": "39de8e8c-9356-499f-b038-1778aa4117c0", "type": "concept", "title": "autos sacramentales"}], "description": "Students analyze how theater and poetry stage authority, social control, and transgression."}]}, {"id": "5d14bdf4-7726-4477-bd74-95c0ab301c16", "type": "topic", "title": "Enlightenment, Neoclassicism, and Romanticism", "description": "This unit follows shifts from reason, reform, and neoclassical order toward Romantic subjectivity, nation, and rebellion. Students connect literature to education, liberalism, gender norms, and political change.", "children": [{"id": "016a56bf-c031-4de2-ac07-367b387ed9e0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Enlightenment reform and didactic literature", "children": [{"id": "1b7c7ab9-ef54-4b50-ae05-f41826ff6c0d", "type": "concept", "title": "Feijoo and essays"}, {"id": "39895f00-1fc2-42d6-aec6-08797679ce6e", "type": "concept", "title": "Jovellanos"}, {"id": "de9faea2-6c66-4c16-aa17-4be0a8a5bdb0", "type": "concept", "title": "neoclassical theater"}, {"id": "2cbbd6c6-6cf9-49f8-8aa9-80821f1e9b56", "type": "concept", "title": "public utility"}], "description": "Students learn how literature participates in projects of reason, education, and social improvement."}, {"id": "ffeb9851-c9ec-4555-8260-1b042555ccc9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Romantic subjectivity and nation", "children": [{"id": "ea578b1c-7175-4148-a034-068acad125d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Espronceda"}, {"id": "c0db153f-949a-4294-b9f0-9bcba6bce3bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Bécquer"}, {"id": "7e74a2aa-34ce-4886-9dea-46acd78bc308", "type": "concept", "title": "Larra"}, {"id": "de988dd3-c7b8-4e58-ae53-c07fdb3e0347", "type": "concept", "title": "liberalism and exile"}], "description": "The point is to understand the Romantic self as emotional, historical, political, and often unstable."}, {"id": "264e5226-55f3-4aa5-ace9-507ac2bc17c8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender and public voice", "children": [{"id": "e46ede3b-c550-4d1f-a697-7838eb4614e3", "type": "concept", "title": "women writers"}, {"id": "49d0fb73-8fc5-4921-b1c8-f1afdf72d0da", "type": "concept", "title": "domestic ideology"}, {"id": "8c49f06d-bc9c-4b63-9ba8-7816e73173ab", "type": "concept", "title": "periodicals"}, {"id": "20f071d9-94ed-4f92-9e01-5e6fccc6bc2b", "type": "concept", "title": "sentiment and authority"}], "description": "Students examine who speaks in public literary culture and under what constraints."}]}, {"id": "e4201ded-54ab-4f1e-b4b9-19aaaea774e8", "type": "topic", "title": "Realism, Naturalism, and Modernity", "description": "The nineteenth-century novel becomes a powerful instrument for mapping society. Students study how realist and naturalist writing represents class, gender, urban life, science, religion, and social change.", "children": [{"id": "06488acb-debe-4065-b6cb-26c4c1161670", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Realist narrative technique", "children": [{"id": "89d7bb5a-3bb6-48a0-a05f-ffc4caf1b4a6", "type": "concept", "title": "Galdós"}, {"id": "2aa43a24-6daf-47ba-baf1-1ed4c100beb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Clarín"}, {"id": "4675f37a-d66d-4fe7-a50e-44c7bab738ac", "type": "concept", "title": "free indirect discourse"}, {"id": "ae6cd3cc-2cf8-4b83-9b9e-4794cfbb5aad", "type": "concept", "title": "urban and provincial space"}], "description": "Students learn how narration, detail, and social observation build fictional worlds."}, {"id": "27821343-ee0a-4c57-afe3-31c7f67c5e99", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Naturalism and determinism", "children": [{"id": "14cb00fe-bf33-4d26-b22d-84ceb34d4b64", "type": "concept", "title": "Emilia Pardo Bazán"}, {"id": "127ef70a-4a5c-4551-882e-b5963056ab5d", "type": "concept", "title": "social environment"}, {"id": "036cbefa-dbb6-4a36-b26e-664b32095270", "type": "concept", "title": "body and illness"}, {"id": "9714bc08-2845-4f3c-a2a9-29ae9238ce71", "type": "concept", "title": "controversy over naturalism"}], "description": "The goal is to examine debates about heredity, environment, science, and morality."}, {"id": "c5dafd41-d419-4ad6-b47d-38f82ccb6301", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modernity and social critique", "children": [{"id": "e9150c6c-b8a0-4799-bc40-696a8c4222e4", "type": "concept", "title": "bourgeois society"}, {"id": "c52afe3a-5a9d-430d-8336-40216345d08a", "type": "concept", "title": "religion and secularization"}, {"id": "5f707838-b380-4b98-b3cc-cda3847712d5", "type": "concept", "title": "gender roles"}, {"id": "db6632d7-6f7f-421f-a086-21fc73a6fc4c", "type": "concept", "title": "education and class mobility"}], "description": "Students connect narrative form to changing institutions and identities."}]}, {"id": "b635c358-59eb-4fb0-96a8-7eaace9bb47e", "type": "topic", "title": "Generation of 1898, Modernismo, and Avant-Garde Spain", "description": "Around 1898 and the early twentieth century, writers confront national crisis and aesthetic renewal. This unit connects intellectual debate, regional identity, modernismo, and avant-garde experimentation.", "children": [{"id": "82c8eaac-b046-49e4-a14c-124ef1a7cec1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "1898 and national reflection", "children": [{"id": "73c73fbe-8e24-4209-b66b-687770c3cac6", "type": "concept", "title": "Unamuno"}, {"id": "ca765bf6-3a66-450f-87ec-25f8fc8497be", "type": "concept", "title": "Azorín"}, {"id": "ff71e883-95b9-4382-a893-dd1939fd7d82", "type": "concept", "title": "Machado"}, {"id": "15206550-4eee-418d-b90d-726b50b9d586", "type": "concept", "title": "Castile as symbol"}], "description": "Students study how literature responds to imperial loss and debates over Spain's future."}, {"id": "3b9d9dd1-b34b-4140-b072-3863a34b8da7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modernismo and aesthetic renewal", "children": [{"id": "63d2b4cc-bc92-4eee-b77c-2d43c18db8c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Rubén Darío's influence"}, {"id": "03a95b4f-5a9b-4f63-a784-4effce248785", "type": "concept", "title": "symbolism"}, {"id": "6087b633-8b67-43fc-8f4d-91bdc37b7b2a", "type": "concept", "title": "musicality"}, {"id": "295e45b6-d889-411e-b93d-49a62d43f9b3", "type": "concept", "title": "cosmopolitanism"}], "description": "The point is to connect Spanish literature to broader transatlantic aesthetic change."}, {"id": "dc6c5ea4-bdfb-41b0-a080-6a6f109d66e1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Avant-garde experimentation", "children": [{"id": "50aeee7c-42ce-4965-8e53-cef8ca9a8c0e", "type": "concept", "title": "ultraísmo"}, {"id": "f088428b-fbad-4f41-96ea-5dfc3f9f7f60", "type": "concept", "title": "surrealist currents"}, {"id": "ec39b92d-b9e9-4293-8992-524cc2c906f3", "type": "concept", "title": "visual form"}, {"id": "ea8bfb8d-143a-4a53-ae39-2144496e4137", "type": "concept", "title": "manifestos"}], "description": "Students see form as a response to modernity, technology, and rupture."}]}, {"id": "cea6bdeb-7110-4f41-94ef-6df1b2c1e2cc", "type": "topic", "title": "Civil War, Exile, Dictatorship, and Memory", "description": "Twentieth-century literature in Spain is marked by war, exile, censorship, trauma, and memory. Students read literature as testimony, resistance, negotiation, and postwar reconstruction.", "children": [{"id": "47bf4cd5-a06a-43fe-9fef-90d204effb76", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Civil War writing and exile", "children": [{"id": "cf8f14dc-6b89-45e9-b3c2-dfc8ee04c2f4", "type": "concept", "title": "poetry of war"}, {"id": "c4585348-76fa-4d01-b20b-676bde7dad93", "type": "concept", "title": "exile communities"}, {"id": "abec0b41-0394-4f4d-9171-04a3538c3dfc", "type": "concept", "title": "Republican memory"}, {"id": "57255ed4-5afe-4eaf-bde0-4099a2afa8f2", "type": "concept", "title": "loss and displacement"}], "description": "Students examine how war and displacement reshape literary voice and political commitment."}, {"id": "6de26a74-18cb-4e34-bbde-3b32fa9a56c7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Francoism and censorship", "children": [{"id": "17821296-7f24-4d0a-a72f-a9d27e8e2aff", "type": "concept", "title": "censorship"}, {"id": "92e342fe-196f-4075-993b-a51913c19a0d", "type": "concept", "title": "allegory and silence"}, {"id": "6705ae31-c319-412a-b501-81dbcb5d8bb5", "type": "concept", "title": "social realism"}, {"id": "2d0e442a-0ebb-4b06-868e-573f0435f686", "type": "concept", "title": "interior exile"}], "description": "The goal is to understand literary production under repression and constraint."}, {"id": "193f9c1f-d72d-4017-8663-f1320812f0ce", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Memory studies", "children": [{"id": "db1745fe-ca58-497c-b746-8d85b6bf1ee9", "type": "concept", "title": "trauma"}, {"id": "f36a17b9-a415-4a94-844a-86f8d399fc05", "type": "concept", "title": "postmemory"}, {"id": "8c2254af-5828-48a0-bb45-f92677eba3b2", "type": "concept", "title": "historical memory"}, {"id": "5950211a-557b-48e7-88d8-db8d0e9f5a68", "type": "concept", "title": "mass graves and public history"}], "description": "Students connect literature to debates over remembering, forgetting, testimony, and justice."}]}, {"id": "9ae7c540-93ea-4977-adc9-01389974fb47", "type": "topic", "title": "Contemporary Spanish Literature and Democratic Spain", "description": "Post-1975 literature reflects democracy, market culture, regional autonomy, migration, globalization, feminism, memory debates, and new media. Students examine contemporary writing as a site of unresolved historical and social questions.", "children": [{"id": "5db0d5d1-7416-4448-ba15-257328c56bf5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Transition and democratic culture", "children": [{"id": "b7205179-5e6c-4b82-87fc-014a3d87fc1d", "type": "concept", "title": "la Transición"}, {"id": "1a9b2694-c5d0-4931-9ff6-6ed8fea5a958", "type": "concept", "title": "Movida"}, {"id": "138d1523-3043-46f8-9445-8aa580eba069", "type": "concept", "title": "memory and amnesia"}, {"id": "9709e339-dd34-4a93-83c6-7ae874ac984c", "type": "concept", "title": "new readerships"}], "description": "Students learn how literature negotiates freedom, forgetting, consumer culture, and political compromise."}, {"id": "4a8abd32-01a3-4b8f-8815-8b985031f8f7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Migration, globalization, and identity", "children": [{"id": "d9245715-2ba0-4fee-9507-9db803767ce4", "type": "concept", "title": "immigration"}, {"id": "e87d1f39-9527-493e-a47a-4258ca160eeb", "type": "concept", "title": "race and belonging"}, {"id": "a665b99b-fe81-4a78-9d25-21c751f78b6f", "type": "concept", "title": "urban peripheries"}, {"id": "1c2d839f-4896-4eaf-8279-523c43953c6d", "type": "concept", "title": "European and Mediterranean contexts"}], "description": "The point is to read contemporary Spain as diverse, contested, and globally connected."}, {"id": "19d31a2a-7e02-4a71-b625-fb470a7873d5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "New forms and media", "children": [{"id": "7ddc5a53-11b1-4fd9-88ee-9e21eff204a3", "type": "concept", "title": "autofiction"}, {"id": "b1b4cda5-fe52-436b-a4b5-ab6fdc3ec5fb", "type": "concept", "title": "graphic narrative"}, {"id": "ca2beb19-26f9-4da4-b5ee-61b258a3388d", "type": "concept", "title": "digital literature"}, {"id": "12b27957-69c9-4555-99e6-111021e00d2d", "type": "concept", "title": "film and adaptation"}], "description": "Students consider how literary production changes with genre, market, and technology."}]}, {"id": "7e374807-4b7c-4cb9-87b2-b24a7ac5d8c8", "type": "topic", "title": "Canon Revision: Women Writers, Regional Literatures, and Marginalized Voices", "description": "This survey teaches the canon and teaches students to question it. This unit asks what changes when women writers, regional languages, class, sexuality, Roma histories, race, and migration are centered.", "children": [{"id": "75697792-35f7-4bed-b38e-16edae0732f9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Women writers and gendered authorship", "children": [{"id": "e2b2c354-e52a-46e0-95c6-948014a86655", "type": "concept", "title": "María de Zayas"}, {"id": "77f213dc-8de3-444e-b8d3-047427f2c85d", "type": "concept", "title": "Emilia Pardo Bazán"}, {"id": "b02c8f61-7fca-46cc-9484-96a1b03866e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Carmen Laforet"}, {"id": "5046e2ca-b9ec-4370-9015-fdff60c50251", "type": "concept", "title": "contemporary women writers"}], "description": "Students recover authors and questions often minimized in older surveys."}, {"id": "53e02f5a-c588-461a-9c40-9dd5cf036ff0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional and multilingual literatures", "children": [{"id": "07f30ad6-005a-466a-a64b-572e29c0a873", "type": "concept", "title": "Catalan contexts"}, {"id": "88a81b5a-bd48-4a60-bca6-7a5fe31e0ef8", "type": "concept", "title": "Galician contexts"}, {"id": "e995c157-2407-4325-893d-86dead4cd92c", "type": "concept", "title": "Basque contexts"}, {"id": "9ef56209-4204-47ea-8351-75d639c330d7", "type": "concept", "title": "translation and self-translation"}], "description": "The goal is to understand Peninsular literature beyond a Castilian-only frame."}, {"id": "c823b6c6-6a03-4743-ab5a-1501b5571feb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Marginalized voices and counter-archives", "children": [{"id": "101c1a78-b03d-410f-bc78-0d3c56a81c4b", "type": "concept", "title": "Roma representation"}, {"id": "659545ca-2ebd-40db-b5a9-3015398a09af", "type": "concept", "title": "class and labor"}, {"id": "56fc8aca-1977-495f-8b25-8781aa2e8367", "type": "concept", "title": "queer writing"}, {"id": "0bbc11d2-7a25-4af1-b3b4-8b514e03682c", "type": "concept", "title": "race, migration, and colonial afterlives"}], "description": "Students learn to ask what sources and histories have been excluded or distorted."}]}, {"id": "baab215b-dd6e-4063-afae-414eda19b76c", "type": "topic", "title": "Historical-Literary Research Paper and Scholarly Presentation", "description": "The research project synthesizes the course: close reading, historical context, source evaluation, and original argument. Students move through stages so the final paper is built, tested, and revised.", "children": [{"id": "6f50873a-950b-4f08-9c45-c2056248ea0d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research question and source discovery", "children": [{"id": "c50cc11f-3d84-40ad-83bb-5db1fff04d5a", "type": "concept", "title": "MLA Bibliography and Dialnet"}, {"id": "47d0fbe5-daa0-4805-8380-3cc21917f7fb", "type": "concept", "title": "digital archives"}, {"id": "0be07bb0-1cd4-441f-bbf8-a6ec52d469fd", "type": "concept", "title": "primary editions"}, {"id": "a67d4c3a-7024-474a-a8a5-99974e239ce5", "type": "concept", "title": "research keywords"}], "description": "Students learn to form a feasible question and locate relevant primary and scholarly sources."}, {"id": "abcdd778-e757-48fc-8b90-b2d3896e8f43", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Argument, evidence, and structure", "children": [{"id": "d7bda9be-2a35-496f-97a7-3f1b8ec5e8a9", "type": "concept", "title": "thesis"}, {"id": "c55218b4-bd24-446e-b53b-1e649f20cbbe", "type": "concept", "title": "close reading evidence"}, {"id": "ba8e70c9-676e-49e3-a431-160f808c7758", "type": "concept", "title": "historical context"}, {"id": "31563938-3bd3-4ae0-b114-159f31546785", "type": "concept", "title": "counterargument"}], "description": "The point is to turn interest into a paper with a claim, stakes, and organized analysis."}, {"id": "2de98d4d-13d6-4d45-bdc9-1b13904a17ac", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revision and presentation", "children": [{"id": "e5f60d2b-1ed0-4ab9-ae8e-3a56b5382064", "type": "concept", "title": "draft workshop"}, {"id": "8261fcb3-0d34-42ed-aa9f-819aac513b8f", "type": "concept", "title": "MLA citation"}, {"id": "1121bd05-5c37-4aed-9a58-e6da0ca6e08b", "type": "concept", "title": "style and register"}, {"id": "9d491626-1c75-46b6-82b5-8f4423ff11a4", "type": "concept", "title": "oral presentation and Q&A"}], "description": "Students practice scholarly communication in written and oral Spanish."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$penljson$::jsonb,
  true,
